      "title": "First day: init, add, commit",
      "lang": "txt",
      "desc": "The problem before\n\nA folder was just files on the desk. There was no hidden attic, no envelope, and no photo. If you deleted a line, it was gone. Sharing meant zipping the folder. Two classmates copied the papers and drifted apart by Friday. Nobody could name snapshot 1. The class notebook had pages but no album, so last Tuesday's homework did not exist except as a rumor in someone's Recycle Bin.\n\nWhat this is\n\ngit init creates the hidden .git attic in that folder so Git can store commits. The working tree is still the files you see. Staging is the empty envelope. git add copies chosen files into that envelope. git commit freezes the envelope into snapshot 1 with a message. GitHub is not required. This is local Git. status tells you what is on the desk versus in the envelope before you click the camera.\n\nWhat it solves\n\nYou have snapshot 1 with a caption, stored in .git, so you can go back. The desk can change after the click and the photo will not. You stopped treating the folder as a pile of loose papers. Later you can add a remote and push the album to a school shelf such as GitHub. First you need the attic, the envelope, and one honest commit. That is the whole first day.\n\nReal-life example\n\nBuy an empty photo album, which is init making .git. Look at the messy desk, which is the working tree. Choose pages for the envelope, which is add. Click the camera, which is commit. The first photo now lives in the attic with a caption such as Add first draft. The school shelf is still empty until you push. You can take photos on a bus with no GitHub at all.\n\nUses\n\nNew projects that are not a clone of someone else's album. Weekend apps, campus labs, and take-home tests that start from an empty folder. Interviews ask what init actually creates: the .git attic, not a website. After the first commit you still use status, add, and commit every day. When you are ready for backup or a teammate, you add origin and push to GitHub or GitLab.\n\nWatch out\n\nRunning init inside an existing repository. You get a nested attic and a confused history. Committing before you read status packs junk. git add . on day one can photograph .env and node_modules. Init does not create a GitHub repo. The shelf is a later step. If you already cloned, you already have .git; do not init again. Nested attics make every later command lie about where photos live.",
      "code": "git init\ngit status\ngit add README.md app.js\ngit commit -m \"Add first draft\"\ngit log --oneline"
