      "title": "Undo uncommitted work",
      "lang": "txt",
      "desc": "The problem before\n\nYou broke a file on the desk and wanted last commit's text back. The zip era had no last photo, only undo in the editor until the editor died. People ran random checkout commands and moved a branch by accident. Staging still held a bad envelope. The class threw the whole notebook away to fix one page. GitHub could not help because the mess was never committed or pushed.\n\nWhat this is\n\ngit restore copies a path from HEAD, the current commit in .git, back onto the working tree. git restore --staged copies from HEAD into staging so the envelope matches the last photo, while the desk can still hold edits. The branch sticker does not move. This is file undo, not history rewrite. GitHub is irrelevant until you push. Old Git said git checkout -- file for the desk restore. You are not creating a new commit.\n\nWhat it solves\n\nThe desk matches the last photo for that file. You keep the branch where it is. You can unstage a secret you added by mistake without deleting the disk copy if you only restore the index. Local oops stays local. Interviews want restore versus reset: files versus stickers. The attic already had the good snapshot. You did not need a new zip. Uncommitted work is the only thing you are throwing away.\n\nReal-life example\n\nThrow the draft page, keep the printed one from the last camera click. The printed one lives in the attic as a commit. The draft was only on the desk, the working tree. If the draft was already in the envelope, restore --staged empties that slot of the envelope back to the printed page. The sticky note on the album does not peel. The school shelf never saw the draft. No new photo is taken.\n\nUses\n\nLocal experiments, botched refactors, and pasted debug dumps you do not want in the next commit. Campus labs after a bad edit. Internships before a demo when you need the last good file. Any time status shows modified and you mean discard. Combine with status so you restore the right path. This is the safe weekday undo for uncommitted work. It is not how you undo a commit already on origin/main.\n\nWatch out\n\nrestore has no recycle bin. Uncommitted desk work vanishes. Restoring the wrong file wipes a good experiment. restore is not reset; you did not move main. checkout -- file in old Git is easy to mix with checkout branch. GitHub cannot restore what you never committed. Stash if you might want the draft later. Clean is a different hammer for untracked files. Read status twice.",
      "code": "git restore src/login.js\ngit restore --staged src/login.js\n# old: git checkout -- src/login.js"
